# game_routes:
# CSES 1681, Game Routes (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1681), in our own words:
# a game has city_count levels and one-way flights between them, no sequence of flights
# leading back to the level it started from; the number of different routes from
# level 1 to level city_count (two routes differ when they use different flights), modulo
# 10^9 + 7. The input is the number of levels, a binary natural
# (corpora/library/binary/natural), and the flights, a Scott list
# (corpora/library/prelude/list) of pairs `pair a b` (corpora/library/prelude/pair), each
# end a binary natural from 1 to the number of levels. The flights have no cycle, as CSES
# promises; the program does not end on a graph that has one. The answer is a
# binary natural. Part of the CSES port's third batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: the list of all routes from a level to
# level city_count is built (the one empty route from level city_count itself, otherwise,
# for each flight that leaves the level, the routes from its end with that end put in
# front), the routes from level 1 are counted in binary and the count is reduced modulo
# 10^9 + 7. Its reading is exponential in the length of the longest chain of flights
# even under tabling, since the routes themselves are built and counted; the replacement the family asks for is the
# dynamic program in topological order, routes(level) = sum of routes(end) over the flights of the level, with
# routes(city_count) = 1, about the levels plus the flights steps, on fixed-width integers modulo
# 10^9 + 7.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application
# only: its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal (the modulus
# and the numerals are written with binary_zero, binary_even and binary_odd). It
# β-reduces to V, the canonical binary numeral of the answer: every recursion
# ends, since the flights have no cycle, so that a route from a level follows each flight
# at most once and has finitely many extensions, and every other recursion
# consumes a finite list or a binary numeral; the branches that a Scott boolean
# discards are never read. ../common/binary_modular's binary_modulo, which writes
# no top, is called with the positive modulus only. So ⊢_β P = V, and P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable
# equations for the contextual preorder of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons append map _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero binary_even binary_odd _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ merge_binary_increment _ _ merge_binary_equality
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "cses") "common") "binary_modular")
λ _ _ _ binary_modulo
. (λtuple. tuple λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd binary_zero)
λ modulus
. (λtuple. tuple (binary_odd binary_zero))
λ one
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple λ. fixpoint λself count items. items count λ_ tail. self (merge_binary_increment count) tail
  )
λ count_from
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ city_count flights
    . fixpoint
      ( λ self level
        . merge_binary_equality level city_count (cons nil nil)
          ( fixpoint
            ( λ walk rest
              . rest nil
                λ flight others
                . flight
                  λ origin destination
                  . merge_binary_equality origin level (append (map (cons destination) (self destination)) λ. walk others) (walk others)
            ) flights
          )
      )
  )
λ routes
. (λtuple. tuple λcity_count flights. binary_modulo (count_from binary_zero (routes city_count flights one)) modulus)
λ game_routes
. export game_routes
